    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1216">

                <pb facs="1203" n="1037" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg1037"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.11/8515</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-22T19:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 22, 1917—7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>660. Simpich reports Yaquis encamped three miles outside settlements, unmolested
                    by troops who, in Consul’s opinion are inadequate to deal with situation, thus
                    exposing forty-five Americans remaining in valley for protection of their
                    property to constant danger of attack.</p>
                <p>Bring to attention of Foreign Office with renewed representations as to necessity
                    for protection of American lives and property and suggest that if, as appears,
                    Sonora troops are unable to extend adequate protection, troops from outside
                    State be promptly dispatched for this purpose.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
